<section style="padding:72px 24px 40px;"><div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;"><div class="rich"><p>Fencing in Irving covers a lot of ground, because the city sits west of Dallas between downtown and DFW Airport and runs from older south Irving streets to the Las Colinas side. The calls are familiar: a 15-20 year old wood fence that has gone grey and started to lean, a section down after a spring storm, a driveway gate that stopped opening, or a new buyer who wants privacy or an iron front fence. This site is advertising for independent fence contractors. It does not build, repair or install anything itself.</p><p>Irving has one rule that catches homeowners out. The city's fence handout says any new or replacement fence needs a permit, and that a contractor, if you use one, must be registered in the City of Irving. Call <strong>(214) 555-0137</strong> now, say which part of Irving the fence is in, and ask for comparable written quotes so you can compare them line by line.</p></div></div></section>
<section style="padding:0 24px 64px;"><div style="max-width:1100px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">What We Do in Irving</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 28px;">Services for Irving Properties</h2>
    <div class="feat-grid" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr);gap:20px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E3DFD8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#2A3035;margin:0 0 8px;">Wood privacy fence installation</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Cedar or pine privacy fencing for Irving back yards, including board-on-board. The city's handout puts rear and side yards at 8 feet, so ask each contractor to quote the height you actually want in writing.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E3DFD8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#2A3035;margin:0 0 8px;">Fence repair</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Leaning posts, missing pickets and storm-damaged sections. Ask the contractor and the City where a repair ends and a replacement begins, because Irving's handout says replacement fences need a permit.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E3DFD8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#2A3035;margin:0 0 8px;">Gate repair and automatic gates</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Sagging walk gates, failed latches and driveway gates that stopped opening. Power wiring for an opener is electrical work, so ask the installer who holds the electrical licence.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E3DFD8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#2A3035;margin:0 0 8px;">Wrought iron fences</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Iron and steel fencing for front and rear yards. Irving's front-yard limit is 48 inches with at least 50 percent see-through, and an open picket style is one way to meet that.</p>
      </div>
</div>
